-- rules/findings.rofl — findings as stimuli to action (the dogfood loop).
-- A finding, once recorded, stays in the open backlog until it receives an
-- explicit disposition: addressed_by(F, Artifact) or dismissed(F, Reason).
-- Silent forgetting is not a state this model can represent: the session
-- start hook renders open_finding into every session's face.
--
-- EDB (facts/findings.rofl):
--   finding(F, Kind)           Kind: insight | pitfall | question | idea
--   recorded(F, Date)          "YYYY-MM-DD"
--   demands(F, What)           What: rule_and_test | kernel_test | doc | decision
--   finding_note(F, Note)      one-line human summary (prose lives in docs/dogfood/)
--   addressed_by(F, Artifact)  path of the rule/test/doc that settles it
--   dismissed(F, Reason)       explicit refusal, with a named reason
--   artifact_removed(F, What)  the artifact that settled it left the tree

settled(F)           :- addressed_by(F, _).
settled(F)           :- dismissed(F, _).
settled(F)           :- artifact_removed(F, _).

-- A SETTLEMENT MUST CITE SOMETHING THAT EXISTS, and for a whole afternoon
-- thirty-three of them did not: `scripts/render_docs.ts --check` walks every
-- `addressed_by` path and one day of deleting had left a third of the closed
-- ledger pointing at bench/, at docs/dogfood/ and at eight scanners. A citation
-- that resolves to nothing is a finding that reads settled and is not, so the
-- disposition is made explicit: the artifact is gone, the NOTE is what remains,
-- and a note is the only thing left to hold the knowledge.
note_lost[audit](F)  :- artifact_removed(F, _), not finding_note(F, _).
alarm(note_lost).

-- AND A FINDING CANNOT BE BOTH REPAIRED AND REFUSED. The sweep above wrote
-- three of these before the file was read back: a finding whose gate was
-- deleted still carried a live `addressed_by` to the repair itself.
two_minds[audit](F) :- addressed_by(F, _), dismissed(F, _).
alarm(two_minds).

open_finding(F)      :- finding(F, _), not settled(F).

-- A FINDING ACCUMULATES NOTES, and until 2026-09-08 nothing said so. Seven
-- findings carry two `finding_note` rows and one carries three, and the second
-- is always a CORRECTION: `DISMISSED THE SAME DAY, and the dismissal is the
-- finding`, `CORRECTED WITHIN THE HOUR, by the owner`, `TRIED IN boot.rofl AND
-- REVERTED, and the negative result is worth more than the idea was`. That is a
-- good convention - a ledger that overwrote its first answer would lose the
-- most valuable half - and `runtime/report.ts` already joins every note, so
-- nothing is hidden from a reader.
--
-- WHAT WAS MISSING IS THAT IT WAS NEVER DECLARED, and an undeclared arity is a
-- trap for the next rule. One was written the same week: a comparison of two
-- ledgers loaded as two perspectives read `finding_note[a](F, X),
-- finding_note[b](F, Y), X != Y` and reported SEVEN findings whose text had
-- diverged between two branches. Not one had. The rule was pairing a finding`s
-- own first note against its own correction, arriving through the other book,
-- and the seven were exactly the seven that carry two. A merge was justified to
-- the owner on that number.
corrected(F)                  :- finding_note(F, A), finding_note(F, B), A != B.

-- ...AND A CORRECTION IS THE MOMENT TO SETTLE OR RE-AIM. A finding that has
-- been revisited and left open is the silent forgetting this ledger exists to
-- prevent, one step subtler: somebody looked, wrote down what they found, and
-- did not say what it changed. Empty on the honest tree, with a planted control
-- in test/findings.test.ts.
corrected_unsettled[audit](F) :- corrected(F), not settled(F).

finding_action(F, W) :- open_finding(F), demands(F, W).

-- The QUEUE, derived rather than narrated. `blocks(A, B)` says A must be
-- settled before B is briefed; until 2026-08-31 the order of work lived
-- nowhere but in my head, so the ledger could say what was found and whether
-- it was closed, and not what to do next. Three rules make the order a
-- consequence of the facts, with a why-tree, and a circular queue would be
-- refused by the same machinery that refuses a circular program.
waiting(F)           :- blocks(A, F), not settled(A).
ready(F)             :- open_finding(F), not waiting(F).
blocked_by(F, A)     :- blocks(A, F), not settled(A).

-- CAUGHT, AND FORBIDDEN TO EXECUTE. Three times in one session I took a
-- DIAGNOSTIC mechanism -- a row in a report that blocks nothing -- and argued
-- about it in the register of DEFENCE, then built a guard for it. Twice the
-- work was reverted: an imperative refusal whose hole was already closed, and
-- a new relation in the meta-kernel for two explained rows of a report. The
-- common step was not carelessness, it was STARTING WORK ON A CLAIM THAT HAD
-- NEVER BEEN RUN.
--
-- `witness(F, Query, N)` already exists for exactly this: a premise stated as
-- a query someone can execute, with the number it returned. Measured when this
-- rule was written: 150 open findings, FIVE with a witness, and of eight open
-- defects exactly ONE. The instrument was built and left switched off.
--
-- So the queue now refuses to offer unproven work. Record whatever you like --
-- an observation costs nothing and the ledger is a journal. But a finding that
-- DEMANDS CODE stays out of `ready` until someone has run the query and
-- written down what it returned. Not a rule about attention: `ready` simply
-- does not contain it, and `unproven` says so by name.
-- A WITNESS HAS FIVE FORMS AND THIS RULE READ ONE, until 2026-09-09. It said
-- `not witness(F, _, _)`, so a finding carrying only `witness_atleast` was
-- refused as unproven while standing on a premise somebody had run. ZERO
-- INSTANCES on the honest tree — both `witness_atleast` rows sit on settled
-- findings, so the hole never fired and nothing would ever have told anyone it
-- was there. It is planted in test/note-witness.test.ts rather than argued.
--
-- This is CLAUDE.md's `orphan_claim` incident verbatim, one book over: a check
-- written by hand for the forms that existed, silently reopened by the next
-- form over the same arguments. The remedy is the same one — derive it ONCE
-- from a relation every form concludes into, so a sixth form costs one clause
-- here and inherits the gate instead of copying it.
-- ALL FIVE ARE DECLARED HERE, and the two old ones were not until today. They
-- were only ever read under a NEGATION - `not witness(F, _, _)` - and
-- `undefined_premise[audit]` is positive-only by design, so an undeclared
-- `witness` was invisible while the ledger that declares it happened to be
-- loaded alongside. `proven(F) :- witness(F, _, _)` is POSITIVE, so this pack
-- standing on its own - the marketplace bundle ships it without the ledger -
-- would have reported a misspelling. Declaring an input is the remedy, not a
-- waiver; boot.rofl says so about `edb(imports)` in the same words.
edb(witness).             -- witness(Id, Query, N)                 -- in w_ledger
edb(witness_atleast).     -- witness_atleast(Id, Query, N)         -- in w_ledger
edb(witness_in).          -- witness_in(Id, World, Query, N)
edb(witness_in_atleast).  -- witness_in_atleast(Id, World, Query, N)
edb(witness_absent).      -- witness_absent(Id, World, Query, Control)

proven(F)            :- witness(F, _, _).
proven(F)            :- witness_atleast(F, _, _).
proven(F)            :- witness_in(F, _, _, _).
proven(F)            :- witness_in_atleast(F, _, _, _).
proven(F)            :- witness_absent(F, _, _, _).

unproven(F)          :- finding_action(F, rule_and_test), not proven(F).
unproven(F)          :- finding_action(F, kernel_test),   not proven(F).

-- THE GATE'S AIM, AS A ROW RATHER THAN AS A SENTENCE IN ITS NOTE. The finding
-- claims the refusal is AIMED, not blanket: a finding demanding a DECISION or a
-- DOC passes untouched, because a decision is made by argument and a query
-- cannot settle it. That sentence was pinned as the COUNT `unproven(F) -> 32`,
-- which moved three times in one night across four branches — twice down by
-- settling, once down again when those two merged, once up by recording — every
-- move correct and the merged number right by accident. A count over the ledger
-- is a reading, not a pin (f_a_count_over_the_ledger_is_a_reading_and_a_floor_is_a_pin).
--
-- This is what the count was standing in for and it is EMPTY ON EVERY BRANCH,
-- so it merges. It is not a tautology of the two clauses above: it goes red the
-- moment somebody adds a third arm over a different `demands` value, which is
-- the only way the gate's aim can actually drift.
unproven_misaimed[audit](F) :- unproven(F), not demands(F, rule_and_test),
                                            not demands(F, kernel_test).

-- and the queue reads it, so an unproven item is not offered as work
ready_proven(F)      :- ready(F), not unproven(F).
